  },
  data: {
    LLM: "There is no strategy. The game is purely mathematical chance with no player decisions affecting the outcome. The only 'choice' is whether to play at a table where the banker's bank is large enough to cover potential payouts.\n\nMoney Management: Since Lansquenet is a 'fast' game with rapid resolution, set a strict loss limit before playing. Historically, experienced gamblers would 'fold' (stop betting) after three consecutive wins to protect their stack.\n\nTheoretical Card Counting: In theory, if playing with a single deck and cards are not reshuffled between rounds, one could track which ranks have been depleted. However, with multiple decks (the standard), this is practically impossible.",
    Player: "There is no strategy. The game is purely mathematical chance with no player decisions affecting the outcome. The only 'choice' is whether to play at a table where the banker's bank is large enough to cover potential payouts.\n\nMoney Management: Since Lansquenet is a 'fast' game with rapid resolution, set a strict loss limit before playing. Historically, experienced gamblers would 'fold' (stop betting) after three consecutive wins to protect their stack.\n\nTheoretical Card Counting: In theory, if playing with a single deck and cards are not reshuffled between rounds, one could track which ranks have been depleted. However, with multiple decks (the standard), this is practically impossible.",
